WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.888 --> 00:00:05.269
- I need to be challenged.

00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:07.548
I need to wake up each
day and break a barrier

00:00:07.548 --> 00:00:10.721
where people tell me I can't do it.

00:00:10.721 --> 00:00:13.138
Call me a freak all you want.

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:17.783
Because of my diverse skill set, yeah,

00:00:17.783 --> 00:00:20.616
I'm a double freak, but I enjoy it.
7
00:00:23,999 --&gt; 00:00:26,904
Hi, I'm Dan Spitz, I
am a master watchmaker.

00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:29.821
I also co-founded the band Anthrax.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:37.873
(heavy metal guitar music)

00:00:39.779 --> 00:00:41.940
Anthrax was one of the
most successful heavy metal

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.440
bands in the history of music.

00:00:46.892 --> 00:00:48.700
We changed the map so
much that people had to

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.688
come up with a term for
it, called thrash metal.

00:00:51.688 --> 00:00:55.170
But, in 1995, I’ve kind of
had enough of touring.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.397
I had children and a family and I wanted

00:00:57.397 --> 00:01:01.314
nothing more to do but
have silence in my life.

00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:06.760
As far as watchmaking goes,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.730
I'm a third-generation watchmaker.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.738
I started taking apart
watches in my grandfather's

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:14.155
jewelry store when I was eight years old.

00:01:15.666 --> 00:01:18.248
After '95, I wanted to get my pedigree and

00:01:18.248 --> 00:01:21.751
be able to take apart what's
considered complications,

00:01:21.751 --> 00:01:24.268
or just crazy avant garde watchmaking,

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:28.019
which is the equivalent of
thrash metal in watchmaking.

00:01:28.019 --> 00:01:31.946
The only way to do that is in Switzerland.

00:01:31.946 --> 00:01:34.269
I was lucky enough to
gain access to a school

00:01:34.269 --> 00:01:36.639
where six people are chosen
from around the world

00:01:36.639 --> 00:01:38.139
every three years.

00:01:41.622 --> 00:01:44.227
As far as the timepieces that I work on,

00:01:44.227 --> 00:01:47.810
the watches usually
start at $50,000 and go

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.215
up to two or three million.

00:01:51.215 --> 00:01:54.913
It is not with a battery,
not with a circuit board.

00:01:54.913 --> 00:01:57.514
You get into two,
three, four thousand pieces

00:01:57.514 --> 00:02:02.460
inside a timepiece that all
have to do certain things.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.964
So, if there's a problem,

00:02:03.964 --> 00:02:07.474
you might have to rip
apart the entire engine.

00:02:07.474 --> 00:02:08.824
We call it a movement.

00:02:08.824 --> 00:02:11.529
Figure out what the problem
is, rebuild the watch.

00:02:11.529 --> 00:02:14.362
There's no road map to the pieces.

00:02:15.877 --> 00:02:17.710
When you're in that zone, in that watch,

00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:19.233
and trying to find that problem,

00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:21.666
when you finally execute whatever that is,

00:02:21.666 --> 00:02:25.180
it's a feeling of euphoria
and it's just like

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.182
when you're in the recording studio,

00:02:28.182 --> 00:02:29.803
just like when you're onstage and you have

00:02:29.803 --> 00:02:33.970
zero room for error and I
take that into my watchmaking.

00:02:37.242 --> 00:02:40.409
(light playful music)

